<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seti I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1290–1279 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Menmaatre, Sethi, Sety Merenptah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seti I was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Nineteenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, ruling from 1290 to 1279 BCE, the son of Ramesses I and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. After the upheavals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s reign he worked to restore order and Egyptian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> several times and took the Syrian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> and the land of Amurru, which soon returned to Hittite control. He built a mortuary temple at Qurna on the west bank at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and began a white limestone temple at Abydos that his son completed. His tomb in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>, KV17, found by Giovanni Belzoni in 1817, is the longest of the New Kingdom royal tombs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seti-i.jpg" alt="Seti I" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keith Schengili-Roberts · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SetiI-KneelingStatueOfferingToOsiris-CloseUp_MetropolitanMuseum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Seti was the son of Ramesses I and Sitre. His name means of Set and indicates that he was consecrated to the god Set. On his accession he took the prenomen Menmaatre, Established is the Justice of Re, and his birth name is transliterated Sety Merenptah, Man of Set, beloved of Ptah. After the social upheavals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s religious reform, the main priority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>, Ramesses I and Seti was to re-establish order and to reaffirm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s sovereignty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which increasing pressure from the Hittite state had compromised. Under his father Seti was made both heir apparent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Seti fought wars in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> in the first decade of his reign, recorded in battle scenes on the north wall of the Hypostyle Hall at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and on several stelae. In his first year he led his army along the Horus Military road, the coastal route across northern Sinai lined with forts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, and fought the Shasu Bedouin there. He also defeated Libyan tribesmen who had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s western border.</p><p>His chief success abroad was the capture of the Syrian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> and the land of Amurru from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. He defeated a Hittite army defending the town, entered it in triumph with his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and set up a victory stela. Kadesh soon reverted to the Hittites, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> could not keep a permanent garrison so close to their homeland.</p><p>At home he completed a chapel at Abydos for his father, whose reliefs show Ramesses I as a deified king associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>. Many of his works were unfinished when he died and were completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. His fame has been overshadowed since ancient times by that of his son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Giovanni Belzoni found his tomb, KV17, in 1817. It is the longest and deepest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> royal tombs, and the first to be decorated with the Book of the Heavenly Cow and reliefs on every passage and chamber, a style that later kings followed. His sarcophagus, carved in one piece, is in Sir John Soane's Museum, which bought it in 1824 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> refused to pay 2,000 pounds.</p><p>Émile Brugsch found his mummy on 6 June 1881 in the Royal Cache at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a>. It was decapitated, probably by tomb robbers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> priests had reattached the head with linen. No evidence of violence was found, and the cause of death remains unclear.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seti I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egyptian sources mention the land in the late Bronze Age: the list of the pharaoh <b>Seti I</b> from c. 1215 BCE, the chronicle of a campaign by Ramesses III, and a letter from a scribe at a border fortress in the Wadi Tumilat under Merneptah reporting &quot;shasu-tribes of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Construction of the Great Hypostyle Hall may have begun in the Eighteenth Dynasty, although most new building was undertaken under <b>Seti I</b> and Ramesses II in the Nineteenth.</blockquote><a href="/work/karnak">Karnak · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
